window.NSS_SEARCH = [{"title": "Nemesis Spawn System — Field Manual", "url": "About.html", "text": "English player reference for the current version, 1.0.3 . Start at Getting started or browse the complete contents . Bosses, features and settings have their own linked pages. Default values describe the current 1.0.3 settings; existing saved settings can differ."}, {"title": "Sound and screen effects", "url": "Atmosphere.html", "text": "Nemesis uses sound, PDA chatter and screen effects to announce pressure and help you read encounters. Effect Where to read more Radio warning Hunts Intercepted chatter Signs before the hunt Directional hunter sound Psy hum Nearby boss cue Boss heartbeat Messages and optional device support PDA messages Lightning and casting cues The Thunder Boss Arrival effects A brief silence precedes the radio warning when enabled. Its default length is one second . Monolith hunts have a call from their arrival position, and a psy screen swell can grow with escalation. Default arrival effect strength is 40% , with a 10% per-cycle increase and a 50% maximum increase . These are presentation settings; they do not add hidden combat damage. Boss presence The normal boss's presence distortion starts within about 60 m , becoming strongest near 5 m . Its default strength is 40% , and it can be felt while he is cloaked. The brief boss strike jolt has been removed and is no longer an available setting. Volume controls General settings separately controls warning, teleport, hum, lightning, heartbeat and arrival-call volumes. A volume of zero mutes the corresponding sound. Lightning volume covers both the Emission Boss's arrival strike and Thunder Boss effects. Wearable Devices can flicker or blank screens during an arrival when that integration is enabled. These effects are optional."}, {"title": "Boss heartbeat", "url": "Boss-Heartbeat.html", "text": "The heartbeat warns that the normal Hunter Boss is close. It begins within about 80 m and speeds up as he approaches. Setting Default Heartbeat volume 60% Heartbeat top rate 140 beats per minute A volume of 0 turns it off. The top rate can be adjusted in General settings . The heartbeat is a proximity cue, not a damage effect. It is separate from psy hum , boss screen distortion and the Thunder Boss's electrical charge sounds. Related: Atmosphere · Cloaking ."}, {"title": "Bosses", "url": "Bosses.html", "text": "Bosses are killable encounters that fight to the death rather than retreating from low health. Each type has its own arrival conditions and mechanics. Boss Default identity Main mechanic Hunter Boss Bogdan Kravets / Lyutomyr Kat / Taras Hrytsenko Cloaked approach and named escorts. Emission Boss Ilarion Hrech Ghosts, a horde and an emission-timed arrival. Thunder Boss Danylo Raskat Storm visits, lightning casting and healing while his hand is raised. Legend A returning hunter's own name A solo boss encounter at the top Returning rank. A hunt's time limit can still end a boss encounter. Only one boss is allowed at a time; individual boss types have further scheduling restrictions. Boss settings include the normal boss chance, escort, reduction, guaranteed misses and stash reward. Emission , Thunder and Returning have their own pages. For credited bodies and stash rewards, see Loot and rewards . Beating a boss hunt halves escalation , rather than applying the ordinary hunt's +1 cycle."}, {"title": "Changelog", "url": "Changelog.html", "text": "Public changes to Nemesis Spawn System , from the first release candidate through 1.0.3 . Version 1.0.3 is the current version covered by this manual. Older entries describe the behavior and defaults at that time; later changes take precedence. For current values, see Settings . For a shorter overview of the current update, see What is new in 1.0.3 . v1.0.3 Added — Thunder Boss Danylo Raskat — A new lone boss in a Loner Exo-SEVA suit, with his own name, settings page and on/off switch. Lightning travel — Five sky strikes within 0.7 seconds mark his arrival and his departure after an unassisted NPC kill. Storm visits — One 15% roll per eligible thunderstorm, after the first hunt, with five in-game days between visits. Storm exclusions — No natural visit during another hunt, an emission or psy storm, or for five real minutes after either event ends. Visible warning — Sparks build around his legs with an electric buzz for 1.5 seconds before he stops and raises his hand. Three volleys — The first follows the raised-hand sky strike by 1.5 seconds; three volleys fire three seconds apart, with two to five bolts each. Keep moving — Player-directed bolts aim half a second behind your movement and take 7% of current health only when they reach you within one metre, with no wall between. NPC targets — Later volleys prefer eligible enemy stalkers or mutants nearest you; the final bolt can kill the NPC target. With no suitable NPC, a volley can target you again. Protected targets — Story NPCs, companions, traders and Nemesis hunters are excluded from his NPC lightning targets. NPC-directed bolts do not damage the player. Casting protection — While his hand is raised, 90% damage reduction replaces the other reductions and their cap; he regenerates 4% of full health per second. NPC resistance — Stalkers and mutants deal 60% less damage to him at all times; player shots do not receive that additional reduction. Between casts — Twenty-second cooldown, with a 50% chance of a 2.5-second inaccurate suppression burst after a cast. Distinct behavior — No cloak, sneaking approach or regular boss's invisible-hunter dialogue; his electrical aura stays visible. Loot — Your kill or credited assist keeps his body and gear; an unassisted NPC kill sends him away in lightning. Thunder controls — Chance, visit interval, name, ranges, damage, casting reduction, regeneration and NPC reduction are adjustable. His settings remain outside difficulty presets. Force Thunder Boss — Added to Force the next hunt; waits until no hunt or boss is active, without needing a thunderstorm. Added — The Returning Remembered hunters — A named hunter forced to retreat by your hits can return by name, with the map of your earlier encounter remembered. Persistent roster — Up to 60 returning hunters are kept per faction, with older entries making room for new ones. Stronger returns — A later hunt has a 35% chance to use a returning leader, one title and game rank higher, with faction-specific dialogue. Modpack rank bands — Uses the game's own rank thresholds rather than hardcoded rank points. Rank effects — AlifeTactics reads the hunter's game rank by default; without it, or when Nemesis is selected, accuracy, vision and burst-length sliders apply per rank. Legends — Hunters reaching the final rank leave ordinary hunts and wait to return alone as bosses, with guaranteed misses, a fight to the death and a stash reward. Legend rolls — Each waiting Legend rolls a 2% chance every two in-game hours, outside active hunts, warnings and boss encounters; the new default boss lockout is 12 hours. Retired names — A killed named hunter's name is retired. Saved dialogue — A returning hunter's last line is remembered across saves to avoid immediate repeats. Returning controls — Own page and switch, return chance, rank-effect provider and sliders, Legend chance, roll interval and boss lockout; difficulty presets include its balance sliders. Debug choices — Force a returning hunter, a Legend, or the Legends' roll. When no Legend waits, the two Legend choices bring a temporary Testing Legend whose death retires no real name. Returning diagnostics — Debug logs list kept hunters, waiting Legends and retired names on load. Added — The Hunt's Eye Intercepted chatter — A PDA line warns of a hunt one in-game hour ahead; the hunt waits for that lead time. Twelve lines per faction, in English and Russian. The Mark — A meter from 0 to 100 grows from credited kills and retreats: boss +20, named hunter +10, regular squad member +2, forced retreat +8. Your actions count — Your kill or assist grows the Mark; NPC-only kills do not. Shorter peace — A full Mark shortens the time between hunts by up to 30%; it does not increase squad size or damage protection. Easing the Mark — It fades over 48 game hours; sleep removes 50% and an anti-psy item removes 35% of the current value by default. Targeted Ground — Each map gains targeting from hunts, forced retreats and time spent there; a fully targeted map brings hunts up to 25% sooner and expands recognition of remembered spots. Targeting defaults — Hunt +15, forced retreat +10, and +10 per 12 hours stayed; other maps cool three times as fast. Leaving and protection — Changing maps drops targeting by 20 on every map; an anti-psy item drops the current map's targeting by 30. Combined limit — The Mark and Targeted Ground together never shorten the hunt interval by more than half. PDA feedback — Messages announce the Mark taking hold, strengthening and fading, and maps becoming targeted or cooling down. Pressure controls — Own page and on/off switch; lead time, gains, fade, relief, targeting and timing effects have individual controls. Disabling it preserves the saved meters. Added — Military hunts Monolith opponents — Monolith players are hunted by the Military by default, with UNISG available as a menu choice; Zombified and Sin players keep UNISG. Military boss — Taras Hrytsenko, with an officer's appearance and a rename box. Military identity — Own squad sections, Corporal-to-Colonel titles and English/Russian radio lines. Emission faction — The emission boss follows the Monolith player's Military/UNISG choice too. Changed — Menu and compatibility Immediate difficulty — A preset applies as soon as it is selected, without Apply; Cancel no longer undoes that selection. Full automatic backup — The backup now includes your pending, unapplied values, allowing it to undo a preset selection too. Nine menu pages — Added The Returning, The Hunt's Eye and Thunder Boss to the existing General, Hunts, Combat, Boss, Emission boss and Debug & tools pages. Wearable Devices PDA — Nemesis messages also reach its Messages page, with a separate General-page switch. Compatibility group — Wearable Devices screen effects and PDA-message controls are grouped together without changing their saved paths. Directional hum — Switched to a mono Yantar psy-voice file so GAMMA's audio replacement no longer turns the hunter's hum into a sound inside your head. Shared Lightning volume — One General-page control covers emission-boss lightning and the Thunder Boss's thunder and bolts. Player guides — English and Russian guides now cover the new features, all nine pages, current settings and both bug-report logs. Exe-script installation — Guides explain the modded exes' db/mods/00_modded_exes_gamedata.db0 archive and older loose-file overrides. Changed — Default settings Medium profile — Rebalanced the Medium preset; existing saved settings are retained. Debug defaults — Difficulty starts at Medium; Debug mode and Debug logs start off. Grace period — 18 → 8 in-game days. Time between hunts — 36 → 24 in-game hours. Hunt time limit — 5 → 6 in-game hours. Named extras — 15% → 25% chance per extra member. Squad weights — Sizes one to five change from 50/25/14/10/6 to 50/40/25/20/10. Retreat health — 30% → 25%. Accuracy boost — 10% → 20%. Armour class bonus — 0 → 0.05. BR mitigation bonus — 0% → 10%. Boss escort — Four → three named hunters. Emission horde — Four → six members. Legend lockout — 36 → 12 in-game hours after a boss's hunt. Anti-psy relief — 100% → 35% removal of the current Mark. Thunder regeneration — 3% → 4% of full health per second while casting. Teleport volume — 40 → 50. Heartbeat volume — 30 → 60. Lightning volume — 80 → 60. Silence length — 0.75 → 1 second. Preset alignment — Easy and Hard are recalculated around the new Medium using the existing step, rounding and range rules; English and Russian default descriptions match. Changed — Logs Separate Nemesis log — appdata/logs/nemesis.log holds the version, errors and, when enabled, diagnostics; it is shared across sessions and rotates past 5 MB. Game-log output — Keeps the version line, important errors and exe-compatibility notices; routine diagnostics use Nemesis's own file. Error visibility — Ordinary errors still reach nemesis.log with Debug logs off; routine diagnostic lines require Debug logs. Bug reports — Request both nemesis.log and xray_<name>.log . Truthful exe warning — Distinguishes genuinely old exes from a new enough exe whose matching scripts are missing or overridden, and includes the build date. Cleanup notices — Normal squad-cleanup notices no longer appear as errors or include a traceback. Silent-hunter diagnosis — Recovery diagnostics appear only with Debug logs; the recovery itself remains active in normal play. v1.0.2.1 — Boss-death hotfix Fixed Boss death crash — Fixed a rare crash when a boss died while pursuit was still evaluating him. Chase error containment — A caught pursuit error is logged and can yield control to normal combat AI. v1.0.2 — 2026-10-22 Added Arrival call — The Monolith's call plays from the arriving squad leader's position, giving the direction of the hunt. Arrival screen effect — A short psy swell accompanies each hunt and a squad following you to another map. Escalating arrival — Each hunt cycle increases the call and swell, with separate growth and cap controls; applied once per squad. Variable heartbeat — A single beat is paced from 85 bpm at 80 m to the selected top rate nearby, with gradual changes rather than abrupt jumps. Silence before warning — Effects and music fade down, pause briefly, then return beneath the radio static; separate switch and duration control. Boss strike jolt — Optional, off-by-default screen jolt when the regular boss finishes his sneak and attacks; removed in 1.0.3. Commented source download — Added an installable -dev.zip alongside the lighter player package, plus a source README, lane register, error guide and MIT license. Changed Emission arrival clock — Waits for the event's fading phase: 200 seconds into an emission, 228 seconds into a psi storm, or five seconds after the event ends. Saved event timing — The event kind and elapsed time survive a save/load; uninstall preparation clears them. Horde before boss — The horde arrives 20 seconds before the emission boss, from outside the ghost ring on his side. Horde flanking — A configurable share of zombies arrives from the opposite door when the shelter has multiple doors. Horde self-defense — Nearby visible enemies count as combat targets despite GAMMA's emission combat limits, without changing other NPCs' rules. Boss retaliation — Removed the five-second player-only target window after your hit; after his approach, the boss can answer whoever shoots him. Boss warning — Radio static alone; removed the extra 2D psy voices that continued into the hunt. Volume restoration — Silence uses temporary runtime factors, restores them on exit and yields when another script changes them; saved volume settings are untouched. Development version labels — Development rounds identify their version separately from release builds. Fixed Double GAMMA damage — With AlifeTactics, NPC hits no longer apply GAMMA's player-hit processing twice; Nemesis protection preserves the existing damage handling. 3DSS and bullet filtering — Silencer wear and the existing one-bullet filter continue to work rather than being bypassed for other NPCs. Strong shots on named hunters — Heavy shots correctly trigger named-hunter retreat instead of bypassing their lethal-hit protection. Artefacts Reinvention perks — Hit/death effects are held only around protected Nemesis hits and applied once when appropriate; surviving hunters do not trigger death perks. Obrien contact — Your shots count as contact and reveal cloaked hunters with Obrien GAMMA Overhaul installed. Knife and grenade reduction — Your non-ballistic hits on regular squadmates receive their damage reduction. Regular armour bonus — Applies even when that regular's other damage reduction is zero. First-hunt pause — Squad assets are warmed on another map shortly after loading, moving their loading work away from the first fight. Horde placement — No longer starts inside the shelter, appears twice in one emission, or spawns on the player's position when the boss cannot land. Load-state leakage — Clears old fight memory, chase slots, siege state and boss focus when loading another save in the same session. PDA sender crash — A message without a sender uses a safe fallback. Ukrainian names — Restores spaces in boss names consistently with English and Russian. Arrival effect cleanup — The arrival swell fades and releases correctly, including when queues end or a save is loaded. Arrival retry window — Waiting for the emission clock no longer consumes the boss's time to find a landing spot. v1.0.1 — 2026-10-21 Added Russian localization — Menu, tooltips, PDA dialogue, system messages, hunter names, ranks and default boss names. Russian player guide — Added Nemesis_README_ru.md to the player package. Startup version — Every game start writes the mod version to the game log, even with debug logging off. Changed Typed names retained — Localized default names do not replace a name already entered by the player. Language fallback — English remains the fallback for other game languages or missing translated strings. Translation provenance — The initial Russian translation was prepared with Claude; wording corrections are welcomed. v1.0.0 — 2026-10-21 Changed First full release — Promoted the completed RC13 haunting and preceding candidate improvements into 1.0.0. xlibs requirement corrected — The mod's verified minimum is 1.9.0, rather than the earlier 1.9.1 requirement. Release documentation — Prepared the player guide, compatibility notes and release descriptions for the first full release. RC13 — Local haunting candidate Changed Large shelters — High-roof halls and buildings with distant walls now qualify as emission shelters. Ghost ring — Twelve ghosts encircle the sheltered position, 55 m out or 25 m beyond a large building's walls. Open ring positions — Ghost positions inside solid ground move to nearby open positions, avoiding gaps. Door barriers — Two or three ghosts stand across each detected door, up to six doors, and push the player back inside. Door detection — Multiple raycasts find low doorways through large or cluttered interiors; high roofs and windows do not count as exits. Push pacing — Ghost pushes cost the configured psy-health share at most once every 1.5 seconds, with psy effects and phantoms. Short-lived phantoms — Reach the player within a second and burst instead of drifting indefinitely. Persistent haunting — Ring and door guards remain until the boss's hunt ends; the boss walks through them. Horde approach — Starts outside the ring and approaches through the door nearest the boss. Sound warm-up — Hunt sounds are loaded after a save load without being played, reducing the first-warning loading work. Removed Closing ghost formation — Replaced drifting door ghosts, the outdoor-only shove and a doorway making way for the boss with the ring and fixed barriers. Fixed Extra-chance documentation — Corrected the stated emission-boss extra chance from 4% to the actual 6%. RC12 — Local 1.0.0 candidate Added Automatic custom backup — Saves custom values before a difficulty replaces them; Load settings from Automatic Backup restores them. Important errors — Selected save/install errors always reach the log in yellow, rate-limited per message. Changed Real shelter required — Ghosts wait for an emission-safe shelter and a short stay inside, rather than any roof. Inward pushes — Ghost shoves move toward the room's middle or are skipped. Immediate boss reveal — Your damaging hit reveals a cloaked boss before the damage lands, preserving earned bodies and assists. Escort after early death — A boss killed during arrival no longer prevents his remaining escort from arriving. Optional Emission Boss — The Emission Boss encounter is included in the same download and can be enabled or disabled independently. Settings pages — Features have their own settings pages while existing saved setting paths and values are retained. Reservation compatibility — Uses the public xlibs body-reservation support when available while preserving existing compatibility. Shared sound handling — Sound effects start at the requested volume through xlibs playback. Fixed Repeated death halving — The saved death consequence is applied once instead of being applied repeatedly. One bullet, one hit — Duplicate impact handling no longer applies a bullet twice, while separate bullets in one frame still count. Debug overhead — Routine logging avoids formatting and other work while disabled. RC11 — Fair fights Changed One damage formula — Armour, type-specific reduction and escalation combine under Max damage reduction for every hunter. Fixed retreat health — Escalation no longer lowers the retreat threshold or increases armour class every cycle. Separate reduction sliders — Added regular, named, boss and emission-boss reductions, escalation step/cap and flat armour/mitigation bonuses. Removed old multipliers — Replaced the older toughness, armour-growth and BR-cap settings with the new damage controls. Boss guaranteed misses — Added a 0–4 control, default two instead of the fixed three, for either boss's first shots at the player. Follow limit control — Added Maps they follow you, default two; zero disables map-following. Complete difficulty presets — Presets now include the balance sliders for normal hunts, map-following, bosses and the emission boss. New Medium timing — Eighteen grace days, 36 hours between hunts, five-hour hunts and an 80–140 m spawn ring. New Medium combat — 15% named extras, 100 m fight range, 30% retreat health, 10% accuracy boost and a 70% reduction cap. New Medium boss values — 4% boss chance, 40% distortion, 6% emission extra chance and hunter attack pull 600. Loot options enabled — Reserve your kills and Boss stash reward became on by default; Lightning strike volume initially defaulted off. Two-second assists — A regular or boss killed by an NPC shortly after your hit counts as your kill, preserving loot and boss rewards. Time-limit retreat — Named hunters, bosses included, flee and teleport when time expires; remaining regulars leave afterwards. Directional psy hum — Moved the hum onto the nearest named hunter as a 3D sound, audible to 100 m. Ghost push rate — Multiple ghosts cannot take psy health more often than once every 0.8 seconds at this stage. Closing door ghosts — Ghosts approach doors from outside; small shelters avoid pushes, and the boss's door makes way for him. Longer haunting — Ghosts remain until the hunt ends or the player changes maps instead of disappearing when the boss dies. Fairer contact — An unseen hostile must be within 15 m to hold a fight; farther away, the boss needs actual sight rather than an automatic player reveal. Staggered background work — Spread slower jobs between updates to reduce recurring stutters. Tagged diagnostics — Log lines identify their subsystem and elapsed time; optional performance lines identify expensive updates. Custom name handling — Limits entered names to 48 characters and restores lost spaces without splitting names such as McAllister. Last-known search — Retained as on by default, on its existing saved setting id. Sniper positioning removed — Removed a firing-position behavior that combat routinely interrupted; snipers use the normal squad movement. Fixed Dead-boss crash — Never restores health, invulnerability or retreat state to a dead hunter, preventing the freeze/crash after shots on a boss's body. Duplicate emission boss — Repeated force selections no longer queue a second emission boss in the same event. Wounded hunters — Named hunters are kept out of the downed crawl that prevented their retreat run. Double NPC reduction — NPC and mutant hits no longer receive a second reduction on named hunters and bosses. Lethal regular hit — Regular damage reduction also protects against the hit that would kill him. Very strong boss hit — Damage larger than full health is no longer truncated before applying the boss's reduction. Duplicate arrival sound — A missing effect no longer falls back to playing the emission's sound a second time. Nil sneak target — Ending a sneak without an NPC id is handled safely. RC10 — Hunters that keep hunting Changed NPC fight limit — After ten seconds without another hit from an NPC or mutant, the hunter returns to pursuing the player. Scheduled handovers — Combat is rechecked after four seconds, 15 seconds, then every 30 seconds, reducing chase/combat oscillation. No enemy, resume chase — Hunters immediately resume pursuit when their selected enemy disappears. Sniper sight rule — A sniper takes a firing position only with sight of the player; out of sight, he keeps moving. Six menu pages — General, Hunts, Combat, Boss, Emission boss and Debug & tools, with grouped options. Automatic Obrien support — Removed the manual armour toggle and its reminder in favor of detection. Unified force control — Replaced separate force checkboxes with Force the next hunt, which acts once and resets to None. Sound controls together — Moved lightning volume to General with the other sounds. Stall diagnostics — With Debug logs, stationary hunters report what controls them and their current target. Removed PDA test checkbox — Removed the standalone test-message control. Fixed Percent formatting — Corrected three emission diagnostic lines that displayed doubled percent signs. RC9 + hotfix — Emission corrections Fixed Plain Anomaly psy damage — Ghost shoves take the configured 4% of the psy bar instead of emptying it in one hit; GAMMA's existing behavior is retained. Emission immunity scope — Dedicated boss sections prevent other Sin or UNISG NPCs from inheriting his emission immunity. Northern horde composition — Late maps receive two tougher snorks rather than one weak and one tough snork. Pending-arrival saves — Saves retain the shelter wait, ghosts, doors, arrival time, horde state and emission roll. Complete uninstall — Uninstall preparation clears the pending arrival, its saved state, custom squads and remaining phantoms. RC9 — Emission boss Added Ilarion Hrech — A Sin emission boss, or UNISG for Monolith, Zombified and Sin players; replaces a hunt during an emission and can rarely arrive between hunts. Haunted shelter — Door ghosts, inward shoves, phantoms and psy-health loss trap the player while the boss approaches. Lightning arrival — Arrives through the emission, with strong NPC resistance and a fight to the death. Emission horde — Zombies, with snorks on northern maps, originally follow 15 seconds after his arrival. Emission controls — Enable, chance, extra chance, name, NPC resistance, horde size, ghost damage, lightning volume and a force-arrival control. UNISG boss identity — Lyutomyr Kat gets his own rename box and faction-aware lines. Reload taunt — Either boss reacts once per hunt when the player reloads nearby. Wearable Devices effects — Device screens flicker on hunt arrivals; boss arrivals add a two-second blackout, with binocular zoom respected. Wearable diagnostics — Optional logs report detection and effect stages without work when disabled. Changed Rare boss stash — Rewards use GAMMA's rare yellow stash tier without consuming the player's own progression. Staggered spawning — Squad members and hordes arrive one per update interval instead of loading an entire group in one frame. Offline timeout — Increased from 90 seconds to five minutes so distant hunters can close in. Random screen flicker — Irregular short gaps replace a fixed three-flash sequence; normal hunts finish with screens on. Fixed Boss name spacing — Names entered without spaces are normalized for display. Obrien warning — Added a PDA notice when the manual armour option is off despite the overhaul being installed. RC8 — Following and retaliation Added NPC retaliation — The boss fights surrounding stalkers and mutants after his stealth approach, with adjustable damage against them. Player priority window — Your damaging hit temporarily makes the boss prioritize you; this window was removed in 1.0.2. Entrance-side following — Pursuers prefer the side of the map entrance, fast-travel arrival or guide drop-off used by the player. Latest-exe features — Optional target bias, attacker redirection and retreat movement hold for supported exes. Attack pull sliders — Separate hunter and boss response strengths; zero leaves target choice to normal AI. Changed Regulars persist — Surviving ordinary squadmates keep hunting after all named hunters leave, and can follow to another map. Hit protection compatibility — Applies protection after other hit modifiers where supported, keeping final damage within the configured Nemesis protection rules. Lower repeated work — Reduced repeated armour, relation and distance calculations during hits and updates. Fixed Smart-cover hits — Named hunters in fixed cover no longer bypass the lethal-hit protection. Following health — Hunters arrive as hurt as they left rather than returning at full health. Dead followers — Killed regulars no longer revive during a map transition. Regular-only saves — Restores hunts with no named hunters left instead of abandoning the squad and allowing a second hunt. Environmental attackers — Anomaly, fire and explosion hits no longer trigger invalid stalker checks during pursuit. Empty spawn squads — Failed spawns queue empty squads for cleanup. Openable stash boxes — Boss rewards exclude inaccessible or invalid stash containers. Saved cloak state — Player-struck bosses keep their reveal state after loading; surviving masked hunters regain their appearance. Formatted debug values — Percent and floating-point values display as numbers rather than raw format strings. RC7 — Named hunters escape Added Boss presence distortion — Screen wave grows as the boss approaches, including while cloaked, with an independent strength slider. Reserve your kills — Protects earned bodies; AlifePlus's own reservation option takes precedence when installed. Regular toughness — Added a flat reduction for ordinary squadmates, independent of escalation. Zombified and Sin support — Uses UNISG opponents for those player factions. Changed Flee before teleporting — A hurt named hunter seeks cover away from the player, or a clear escape lane, before disappearing. Named bodies vanish — Named hunters never leave loot bodies; regulars and the boss retain the player-kill loot rule. Equal protection — NPC and mutant hits receive the same mitigation and cap as player hits. Psy hum renamed — Replaced the whisper setting with Psy hum volume and revised its distance/volume behavior. Fifteen-second debug warning — Debug hunts arrive a fixed 15 seconds after the warning. Single active hunt — No new hunt or warning while a squad is still hunting or following between maps. Fixed False retreat cover — A hunter's current position no longer counts as a successful escape run; selected cover must be farther from the player. Knockdown at retreat — Restores engine health in the requesting hit so the hunter can flee instead of dropping wounded first. NPC lethal hits — Strengthened the pre-hit floor and reduction so NPC shots do not skip retreat protection at later cycles. Late death notification — Correctly removes NPC-killed bodies even when the update notices death before the callback. Visible death frame — Masks bodies scheduled to vanish before the killing hit where appropriate. Missing psy sound — Uses an existing sound file and checks sound availability before playback. Cloak scan warnings — Filters unsuitable objects during cloak checks, avoiding repeated engine error messages. RC6 — Ballistics hotfix Fixed NPCs could not kill each other — Stopped a player-bullet duplicate filter from cancelling repeat NPC-on-NPC hits. Double player hits — Removed duplicate GAMMA ballistics application on named hunters by wrapping the registered handler correctly. RC5 — Boss fights to the death Added Boss stash reward — Optional stash marked after the player's boss kill; initially an ordinary stash and off by default. Clear menu groups — General, Gameplay, Combat, Boss and Debug, with timing, squad, behavior, damage, armour and reward groups. Changed Mortal boss — Removed the boss's retreat floor; he fights until killed and leaves earned loot. Player-kill bodies — Bodies earned by the player remain, while kills by others follow the teleport-removal rule. Player breaks siege — Shooting a hunter from inside makes the squad storm in immediately. Boss moves indoors — After his approach, the boss enters rather than taking a siege waiting position. Shorter escort siege — In a boss hunt the escort waits 20 seconds outside instead of 90. Cloaked neutrality — Extended the boss's ignore/ignored rule to other cloaked hunters; damaging hits reveal them. Attacker response — Hunters recognize an NPC shooting at them, including recent hits and available under-fire memory. Regular boss response — Once his approach ends, visible contact can bring him into combat instead of leaving him running at the player. Fixed Resetting siege timer — Brief indoor/outdoor fluctuations no longer restart the full wait; ten seconds outside is required. Boss that never fired — Removed siege interference and corrected combat handover after his approach. Ignored player siege shots — Your hit ends the wait even before the hunter's normal combat AI has selected an enemy. RC4 — Escalation and map pursuit Added Boss chance ramp — One roll per hunt, +2 percentage points every four hunts without him, guaranteed by the 30th eligible hunt. Max damage reduction — New cap on the combined protection, initially 80%. Area scaling — Smaller squads in rookie areas and more named extras in late areas, with an option to disable scaling. Underground exclusion — Hunts do not start in underground levels. Remembered positions — Keeps up to five player locations per map for seven game days; returning there improves pursuit and flanking. Indoor siege — Hunters wait around the building for 90 seconds before storming in; the boss follows his own approach. Two-map following — Survivors follow across level changes, shortcuts, fast travel and guides; a further crossing can shake them off. Changed Escalation outcomes — Beating a normal hunt adds a cycle; outlasting it removes one; beating the boss or being killed by a hunter halves the cycle. No daily decay — Removed passive escalation decay and its setting. Movement detection — Reads movement flags directly, avoiding false running from teleporting or changing levels. Following state — Carries names, faction, boss identity and health between maps; the hunt's time limit continues while they follow. RC3 — Stealth boss and safer control Added Prepare for uninstall — Ends hunts, drops pending warnings and removes Nemesis squads before the player saves and uninstalls. Boss escort control — Adjustable named escort, independent of normal squad weights. Boss extra armour — Separate boss-only armour-class bonus, later replaced by RC11's unified controls. Boss arrival tell — The boss and escort arrive in teleport bursts with positional sound. Debug boss marker — Optional boss map marker for debug encounters. Changed Movement-aware sneak — The boss circles behind a stationary player and pursues a running one, then homes in without repeatedly circling outward. Telegraphed attack — Different close, behind and running triggers announce the attack before firing. Cloaked boss neutrality — Other NPCs ignore him while he approaches; the player's damaging shot reveals him. Guaranteed player misses — The boss's initial miss allowance counts only shots at the player, not shots at NPCs. Hostile bases — Enemy-controlled bases no longer prevent a hunt; friendly and neutral bases still do. Safe uninstall guide — Documents why saves made with an active hunt require cleanup before removing the mod. Removed Last-seen map markers — Retiring hunters no longer place a temporary red marker on nearby smart terrains. Fixed Rotating-player approach — Looking around no longer sends a homing boss back to the outer circling ring. RC2 — Difficulty and contact Added Difficulty presets — Easy, Medium, Hard and Custom, with the initial group of balance sliders. Accuracy boost — Named hunters gain the selected accuracy bonus, regulars half and the boss an additional bonus. Separate warnings — Independent arrival-audio and PDA switches; radio on and PDA off by default. Changed Immediate preview — Selecting a preset updates its sliders before Apply; Apply saves it and Cancel drops the preview at this stage. Six-hour limit — Changed the default hunt duration from four to six hours to match Medium. Boss delay — Default days after grace increased from three to seven and joined the presets. Contact before combat — A hunter seeing or being hit by the player stops and faces them while normal combat acquires its enemy, instead of sprinting straight into them. Combat linger — Keeps combat briefly after contact, then resumes pursuit when the fight has ended. Shared cleanup — Improved end-of-hunt cleanup and teleport sequencing. Missing required dependencies disable hunts with a clear log message. Fixed Main-menu difficulty — Preset selections made before loading now reach the apply handler. First preset selection — A deliberate first pick is applied rather than incorrectly becoming Custom. First-open preview — Retrieves MCM's active window correctly so the preview works on its first opening. Cover reservation leak — Frees reserved cover on deaths, vanished objects and other exit paths. RC1 — First public candidate Added Recurring hunts — Named Monolith hunters and optional ordinary squadmates spawn out of sight and pursue the player. Arrival warning — PDA message and radio static precede the hunt; named hunters use cycle-dependent taunts and later address the player by name. Persistent names and ranks — Names appear on the NPC itself, with faction titles distinguishing named hunters from ordinary squadmates. Escalating threat — Beaten hunts strengthen later ones, with armour-based protection and configurable retreat thresholds. Retreat and teleport — Wounded named hunters flee toward cover, disappear in a burst and are removed; ordinary squadmates can be killed. Grace and timing controls — New-game grace period, hunt interval, hunt time limit, spawn ring, squad weights and named-extra chance. Safe start conditions — Bases, dialogue and sleep delay hunt starts; already announced hunts remain committed after entering a base. Memory and tactics — Last-known-position pursuit, alternating flanks, retreat cover and cooperation with AlifeTactics takeovers. Experimental hunter cloak — Named hunters become invisible while stalking from range, revealing on combat, proximity or hits. Bogdan Kravets — A rare tougher boss with a distinct appearance, cloak, approach, warning lines and renameable identity; his behavior was expanded in later candidates. Faction-aware opponents — UNISG hunts Monolith players, with its own appearance and dialogue. Psy ambience — Proximity voices, teleport sound, PDA icon and optional HD portrait. Debug controls — Accelerated hunts, detailed logs and manual escalation reset. Save/load support — Preserves hunt progress, member state, names and pending teleports; completed hunts clean up their empty squads. Compatibility foundations — Dedicated Nemesis files, reliable pursuit, safe squad cleanup and protection from GAMMA's southern Monolith cleanup. Fixed before the first candidate Squad spawn validity — Hunt members no longer use unsuitable spawn sections that the engine removes. Online/offline chase — Reinstalls the chase after online objects are recreated instead of leaving hunters without pursuit. Self-defense — Allows hunters to fight nearby threats rather than running helplessly through them. Health assignment — Fixed damaging hits accidentally healing a hunter. Looting while chasing — Blocks loot/help planners during pursuit and avoids smart-terrain jobs taking over the hunters. Multiple deaths — Completes all same-frame deaths before ending a hunt. Lower update cost — Caches settings and reads the selected enemy instead of repeatedly scanning all nearby objects. Recycled NPC ids — Unrelated NPCs no longer inherit Nemesis armour or hunter state when the game reuses an NPC ID. Teleport persistence — Finishes pending removals after loading rather than leaving invisible survivors behind. Dead-model crash — Avoids changing a corpse's visual while its physics are being built."}, {"title": "Chasing and combat", "url": "Chasing-and-Combat.html", "text": "Hunters alternate between pursuing you and fighting through the game's combat AI. They sprint from a distance and approach with their weapon ready when closer. Finding you Hunt your last known position , on by default, lets hunters search the place where you were last seen, heard or involved in a hit. Turn it off and their pursuit heads directly toward your current position. Remembered ground can reveal your position near an old fighting spot. Targeted Ground can widen that remembered area. Handing over to combat The default Fight range is 100 m. Seeing you and selecting you as an enemy allows the hunter to hand over to combat. A hunter who has seen you before his combat AI fully reacts can stop and face you rather than simply sprint into you. Hunters can also fight hostile stalkers or mutants. Ordinary hunters eventually leave a distracting NPC fight and resume the chase unless the threat keeps engaging them. The normal boss has his own approach before ordinary combat takes over. With Squad tactics enabled, extras can approach from flanking sides. A chasing hunter does not stop to loot bodies or help wounded NPCs. Optional AI integration AlifeTactics can supply its usual combat behaviour and rank effects. Latest exe AI features enables supported targeting and retaliation behaviour; the Hunter and Boss attack-pull settings influence how readily they turn toward a shooter. These settings do not mean Nemesis replaces all combat AI. Bosses add encounter-specific actions such as the Thunder Boss's cast . Related: Sieges · Cloaking · Combat settings · Hunts settings ."}, {"title": "Cloaking", "url": "Cloaking.html", "text": "Named hunters Hunter cloak (experimental) is on by default. Named hunters can become invisible while stalking from a distance. They can cloak beyond 30 m , and normally reveal within 15 m , when fighting, or after a hit. Cloaking has a 20-second cooldown between applications. Regular squadmates do not cloak. Turning off Hunter cloak disables the optional named-hunter cloak; the Hunter Boss's special cloaked approach has its own rules. The Hunter Boss The normal boss cloaks for his approach. Merely looking toward him does not cancel the sneak. A shot from you breaks his approach and keeps him visible for the rest of that hunt. Proximity and attack conditions also end the approach as described on his page. The Emission Boss and Thunder Boss do not use this sneak-and-cloak encounter. Other NPCs Cloaking also makes affected stalkers ignore the cloaked hunter, and the cloaked hunter ignores them during the approach. It does not change the player's relation to him. The invisible model must be available for the visual effect. See Compatibility if cloaking is absent or behaves differently in a modified installation. Related: Psy hum · Boss heartbeat · Hunts settings ."}, {"title": "Compatibility", "url": "Compatibility.html", "text": "Required components Component Requirement Game Anomaly 1.5.3 with MCM, or GAMMA. xlibs 1.9.0 or newer, enabled. Modded exes Current themrdemonized release with matching scripts active. The necessary callback features date from 15 September 2026 . Their scripts are supplied in db/mods/00_modded_exes_gamedata.db0 . Conflicting loose scripts can override them even if the executable itself is new enough. Do not infer that a dependency is enabled from its presence on disk; check your mod manager. Exact mod-list names vary. Optional integrations Mod/system What Nemesis provides AlifePlus Hunt-squad ownership compatibility and coordination with its kill-reservation option. AlifeTactics Normal combat coexistence and an optional rank-effect source for Returning hunters. GAMMA ballistics / Obrien GAMMA Overhaul Protection handling for Nemesis hunters alongside the player's ballistics chain. GAMMA Psy rework Ghost psy damage through the rework's psy-health system. Wearable Devices Optional arrival flicker and boss screen blanking. Wearable Devices PDA mod Optional forwarding to its Messages page. Interaction Dot Marks Removal of stale markers for Nemesis bodies/weapons that disappear. GAMMA No Exos in the South Living hunt members are exempt while the hunt runs. These integrations do not mean the optional mods are required. Nemesis does not redistribute Wearable Devices files or assets. Appearance and AI differences Cloaking requires the invisible stalker model to be available. If it is missing, do not expect the same invisible appearance. Other mods' combat restrictions, safe zones and loot tables still matter. See Faction variants . For missing dependencies, unexpected named-hunter deaths or missing messages, see Troubleshooting ."}, {"title": "Wiki contents", "url": "Contents.html", "text": "English · Current version: 1.0.3 Start here Nemesis Spawn System — Field Manual Getting started Installation and updating Uninstalling What is new in 1.0.3 Changelog Hunts and hunters Hunts Hunters and squads Faction variants Chasing and combat Retreats Escalation and protection Cloaking Sieges Remembered ground Map following Boss encounters Bosses The Hunter Boss The Emission Boss Ghosts The emission horde The Thunder Boss Legends Progression and pressure The Returning The Hunt's Eye Signs before the hunt The Mark Targeted Ground Rewards and atmosphere Loot and rewards Reserve your kills Sound and screen effects Psy hum Boss heartbeat PDA messages Configuration Settings Difficulty and presets General settings Hunts settings Combat settings Boss settings Emission Boss settings The Returning settings The Hunt's Eye settings Thunder Boss settings Debug & tools settings Help and reference Compatibility Troubleshooting Frequently asked questions Credits"}, {"title": "Credits", "url": "Credits.html", "text": "Phoenixxx : the original idea for this mod. Damian Sirbu : xlibs , the library this mod is built on. xlibs by Damian Sirbu https://github.com/damiansirbu-stalker themrdemonized : the modded exes ( xray-monolith ), the engine this mod is written for. SirEndet : design, testing and direction. Built with AI assistance (Claude by Anthropic). About this manual This English Field Manual describes the public features and settings of the current version, Nemesis Spawn System 1.0.3. Related: Home , Getting started , Compatibility ."}, {"title": "Difficulty and presets", "url": "Difficulty-and-Presets.html", "text": "Difficulty is selected at the top of General : Easy, Medium or Hard. Medium is the default for the current version, 1.0.3. Changing a controlled value yourself can make the selection show Custom. Immediate application and backup A preset applies as soon as you select it . It does not wait for Apply, and Cancel does not undo it. Before a preset replaces your settings, Automatic Backup preserves your own values, including pending slider edits. Load settings from Automatic Backup restores that saved set and can undo the preset choice. Presets affect the values listed below. Check boxes, volumes, presentation effects and Thunder Boss settings are left alone. Signs lead is the same across difficulties. Do not assume every number is a universal percentage adjustment; use the exact table. Preset comparison Setting Easy Medium Hard Grace period (days) 9 8 7 Time between hunts (hours) 28 24 20 Hunt time limit (hours) 4 6 8 Extra is a named hunter (%) 20 25 30 Maps they follow you 1 2 3 Squad of 1 to 5, weight 60 / 48 / 25 / 16 / 8 50 / 40 / 25 / 20 / 10 40 / 32 / 25 / 24 / 12 Retreat health (%) 30 25 20 Accuracy boost (%) 16 20 24 Regular stalker damage reduction (%) 0 0 0 Named hunter damage reduction (%) 20 25 30 Escalation per cycle (%) 1.5 2 2.5 Max escalation (%) 15 20 25 Max damage reduction (%) 55 70 85 Hunter armour class bonus 0 0.05 0.1 Hunter BR mitigation bonus (%) 5 10 15 Boss chance (%) 3 4 5 Boss days after grace 7 7 6 Boss escort size 2 3 4 Boss damage reduction (%) 20 30 40 Boss damage against NPCs (%) 150 200 250 Boss guaranteed misses 3 2 1 Emission boss chance (%) 80 100 100 Emission boss extra chance (%) 4 6 8 Emission boss damage reduction (%) 20 30 40 Emission boss NPC damage reduction (%) 55 70 85 Emission boss horde size 4 6 8 Horde flank share (%) 20 30 40 Ghost psy damage (%) 3 4 5 Return chance (%) 25 35 45 Accuracy per rank (%) 3 4 5 Vision per rank (%) 3 4 5 Bursts per rank (%) 4 5 6 Legend chance (%) 1 2 3 Legend roll every (hours) 3 2 1 No Legend after a boss (hours) 14 12 10 Mark per boss kill 16 20 24 Mark per named hunter kill 8 10 12 Mark per squad member kill 1 2 3 Mark per forced retreat 6 8 10 Mark: hunts sooner (%) 20 30 40 Mark fades over (hours) 38 48 58 Sleep eases the Mark (%) 60 50 40 Anti-psy eases the Mark (%) 40 35 30 Targeting per hunt 12 15 18 Targeting per forced retreat 8 10 12 Targeting per stay 8 10 12 Targeting stay (hours) 14 12 10 Cooling rate (x) 4 3 2 Targeting drop per map travel 25 20 15 Targeting drop per anti-psy 35 30 25 Targeting: hunts sooner (%) 20 25 30 Targeting: they know your ground (%) 80 100 120 The five squad-size weights share one row. They are relative weights and do not need to add to 100. Existing games The current 1.0.3 defaults do not automatically overwrite existing saved values. Select Medium deliberately to apply its preset-controlled values. Review other pages separately for values outside the presets. Related: Settings · General settings · Escalation ."}, {"title": "Ending a hunt", "url": "Ending-the-Hunts.html", "text": "Beating the squad An ordinary hunt ends when all its members are dead or gone. Named hunters can retreat, but surviving regular squadmates continue hunting. Killing a boss does not immediately end the encounter if its escort is still active. Escaping Surviving the hunt time limit counts as an escape. Travelling beyond the configured map-follow allowance also shakes off the squad. See Map following for its limits and delays. A beaten ordinary hunt, a beaten boss hunt and an escape have different effects on escalation . Removing the mod For permanent removal, use Prepare for uninstall , wait for the safe-to-save message and save before disabling Nemesis. Related: Hunts · Retreats · Loot and rewards ."}, {"title": "Escalation and protection", "url": "Escalation-and-Protection.html", "text": "Escalation is the persistent hunt cycle. It is separate from Returning ranks , The Mark and Targeted Ground . How the cycle changes Outcome Cycle change Ordinary hunt beaten +1, regardless of whether you or other NPCs finished its members. Boss hunt beaten Halved, rounded down, never below 1. Hunt escaped through its time limit −1, never below 1. Hunt escaped by exceeding map-follow allowance −1, never below 1. Killed by a hunter, with death halving enabled Halved, never below 1. The death consequence can survive reloading an older save. Killing the boss while his escorts are still fighting does not immediately finish the whole hunt. The encounter remains a boss hunt when its final outcome is decided. Escalation does not fade simply because time passes. Damage protection The ordinary protection calculation combines armour mitigation, the member type's damage reduction and escalation. Named hunters and bosses can receive 2% escalation protection per cycle after the first , capped at 20% by default. Regular squadmates do not receive this escalation protection. Setting Medium default Regular stalker damage reduction 0% Named hunter damage reduction 25% Hunter Boss damage reduction 30% Emission Boss damage reduction 30% Maximum ordinary damage reduction 70% Hunter armour class bonus 0.05 Hunter BR mitigation bonus 10% Armour class and percentage mitigation are different. In GAMMA, the relevant ballistics protection can depend on the player's equipment; named hunters and bosses copy protection where supported, while regulars keep their own armour. Do not add the listed percentages as if they were all one flat reduction. Thunder is an exception: his 90% casting reduction replaces the ordinary reductions and their cap while casting. He also has a separate NPC damage reduction setting. See The Thunder Boss . Settings: Combat · Boss · Emission Boss · Thunder Boss ."}, {"title": "Frequently asked questions", "url": "FAQ.html", "text": "Why will this hunter not die? Ordinary named hunters have protection intended to make them retreat . Regular squadmates and bosses are killable. Does clearing a hunt make the next one harder? An ordinary beaten hunt increases the cycle. A beaten boss hunt halves it. Timing out or escaping beyond the follow allowance lowers it by one. See Escalation . Can I hide in a base? A friendly/neutral base can prevent a normal hunt from starting. Entering one after the arrival warning does not cancel the hunt. See Hunts . Can I escape by changing maps? Hunters can follow twice by default. Going beyond the allowance escapes the hunt. A follow allowance of zero makes map travel an escape. Why did Medium not automatically replace my old settings? Existing saved values remain yours. Select Medium deliberately if you want that preset. It applies immediately; Cancel does not undo it. Why did the Thunder Boss ignore my difficulty choice? His settings are outside the difficulty presets. Use Thunder Boss settings . Why did a returning hunter become a solo boss? He reached Legend . Top-rank Returning hunters leave ordinary hunts and wait for that encounter. Does the Mark grow because I took damage? The Mark grows from credited kills and forced retreats against Nemesis members. See The Mark . Does an anti-psy drug remove 35 Mark points? It removes 35% of your current Mark by default. Targeted Ground uses its own point reductions. Do I need both downloads? No. The player and commented-source packages have the same gameplay. Install one. See Installation . Can I uninstall during a hunt? Use Prepare for uninstall , wait for confirmation, then save before removing the mod."}, {"title": "Faction variants", "url": "Faction-Variants.html", "text": "Nemesis chooses hunter factions according to your player faction. Player faction Ordinary hunter faction Emission Boss faction Most factions Monolith Sin Monolith Military by default; UNISG can be selected Military or UNISG, following the same selection Zombified or Sin UNISG UNISG The Monolith players are hunted by option is on Hunts settings . A change applies from the next hunt. Boss identities Hunt faction Hunter Boss default name Monolith Bogdan Kravets UNISG Lyutomyr Kat Military Taras Hrytsenko These names can be changed in Boss settings . The Emission Boss has his own name setting, Ilarion Hrech by default. The Thunder Boss is Danylo Raskat by default. Related: Hunters and squads · Compatibility · Bosses ."}, {"title": "Getting started", "url": "Getting-Started.html", "text": "Requirements S.T.A.L.K.E.R. Anomaly 1.5.3 with MCM, or GAMMA. Current modded exes by themrdemonized , with their matching game scripts active. xlibs 1.9.0 or newer , enabled in your mod manager. The required callback features were added on 15 September 2026 . Use a current engine release rather than treating that date as the recommended version. An updated executable alone is insufficient if conflicting files override its scripts. See Compatibility . Your first hunt With Medium defaults, a new game has eight in-game days of grace. A normal hunt then has a 24-hour cooldown after the previous hunt. The Hunt's Eye can shorten that wait later. An intercepted Sign gives an early warning. Closer to arrival, radio static announces the hunt. A squad normally appears 80–140 metres away, out of sight, and heads for you. A normal hunt does not begin while you are underground, asleep, in dialogue, or near a friendly or neutral base or trader camp. This protection does not cancel a hunt once its arrival warning has already been issued. The rules worth learning Named hunters usually retreat instead of dying to ordinary damage. Regular squadmates and bosses are killable. Clearing an ordinary hunt makes the next one stronger. Surviving its time limit makes the next one weaker. See Escalation . Moving to another map does not necessarily escape the squad: hunters can follow . Buildings buy time, but sieges can turn into an assault. Bosses have their own rules. Learn the Hunter Boss , Emission Boss , Thunder Boss and Legends separately. Adjusting the experience Open MCM → Nemesis Spawn System . Difficulty presets apply immediately. Thunder Boss settings are independent of the presets. Debug mode and Debug logs are off by default. For installation, updates and removal, follow Installation and updating and Uninstalling ."}, {"title": "Ghosts", "url": "Ghosts.html", "text": "Ghosts belong to the Emission Boss encounter . They hold you in an emission shelter while the encounter develops. The ring and exits The haunting places a ring of 12 ghosts around your shelter, normally at roughly 55 m . Large buildings can move that ring farther out, about 25 m beyond the farthest wall . Additional groups of ghosts guard detected doors, up to six exits. The door search tries to distinguish low door openings from windows. Complex geometry can still make the encounter depend on the actual shelter shape. Pushes and psy damage Getting too close to the ring or an exit ghost pushes you back. Door ghosts normally react within 3.5 m . Each eligible push costs 4% of your full psy bar by default, regardless of ordinary protection, at most once every 1.5 seconds . In a very small shelter, door ghosts do not push; the outer ring still applies. Standing outdoors inside the ring does not make the emission safe. The ghosts are not an alternative route out of the emission. Stay in the actual shelter and prepare for the horde and boss. Ending the haunting The ghosts persist until the encounter ends or you change maps. They do not stop the boss from entering, and following him onto another map does not create a new haunting there. Settings: Ghost psy damage . Related: Sieges , which use a different indoor check."}, {"title": "Hunters and squads", "url": "Hunters-and-Squads.html", "text": "Named hunters and regulars Type Behaviour Named hunter Has a title and name; protected from ordinary lethal damage and retreats when sufficiently hurt. Can progress through The Returning . Regular squadmate A killable stalker. Can continue the hunt after named members leave. Does not gain the named hunters' escalation protection. Boss A separate, killable encounter leader with boss-specific behaviour. See Bosses . A normal squad has one to five stalkers. Its first member is named. Each extra has a 25% base chance to be named, with regional adjustments when enabled. Squad-size weights Squad size Default weight 1 50 2 40 3 25 4 20 5 10 These are relative weights, not percentages, and do not need to total 100. Boss escorts use their own size setting. Area scaling With Scale hunts by area enabled, low-danger areas cap normal squads: tier 0 areas such as Cordon and Swamps allow at most two members, and tier 1 areas such as Garbage allow at most three. Higher-danger areas increase the chance of named extras: +10 percentage points at tier 4, +20 at tier 5. Unknown maps use the neutral middle tier. Boss hunts are excluded from this scaling. Titles and identity Titles follow the hunter faction . With The Returning enabled, a fresh named hunter starts at the game's Experienced rank, then climbs through Professional, Veteran, Expert and Master before entering the Legend stage. Titles describe the returning hunter's progression; they are distinct from escalation, which belongs to the hunt cycle. A regular stalker with a personal name is still not necessarily a named Nemesis hunter. Related: Retreats · The Returning · Loot and rewards · Hunts settings ."}, {"title": "Hunts", "url": "Hunts.html", "text": "A hunt is a recurring squad encounter that follows you until it is beaten or you escape. The normal sequence Stage Medium/default behaviour Grace Eight in-game days before the first hunt of a new game. Cooldown 24 in-game hours after an encounter; pressure can shorten the effective wait. Early warning Signs normally give one in-game hour of notice. Arrival warning Radio static, plus an optional PDA warning, shortly before spawning. Arrival A squad normally appears 80–140 m away. Encounter Hunters chase, fight, flank, retreat and can follow across maps. Time limit Six in-game hours by default. The normal arrival warning precedes spawning by a random 30–120 seconds . Debug mode changes normal timing; it is not the default experience. Where hunts can start A normal hunt is held back while you are asleep, in dialogue, underground, or near a friendly/neutral base or trader camp. The base check uses its current relationships: an enemy base is not a refuge. Once the arrival warning has been issued, entering a friendly base does not cancel it. Dialogue or sleep can delay arrival. A running hunt can enter a base after you, although other mods' safe-zone rules can still affect NPC combat there. Spawn placement avoids bases themselves. Squad composition is described in Hunters and squads . Ending a hunt An ordinary hunt is beaten when its members are dead or gone. Surviving regular squadmates continue after the named hunters retreat. Running out the time limit counts as an escape. Exceeding the configured map-follow limit also counts as an escape. The outcome changes escalation . A boss hunt remains a boss hunt even if its boss dies before the escort is cleared. Only one hunt runs at a time, including a squad waiting to follow. Boss encounters use additional arrival rules: Hunter Boss , Emission Boss , Thunder Boss , Legends . Related settings: Hunts · Combat · The Hunt's Eye ."}, {"title": "Installation and updating", "url": "Installation-and-Updating.html", "text": "Choose a package Package Contents NemesisWaveSpawnSystem_1.0.3.zip The lighter player package, with explanatory script comments removed. NemesisWaveSpawnSystem_1.0.3-dev.zip The installable commented-source package. Both packages provide the same gameplay. Choose one; you do not need both enabled. Update the modded exes first Close the game. Open the latest official modded-exes release and download the packaged ZIP for your engine variant (standard or MT). Extract the complete archive into your Anomaly game folder , alongside AnomalyLauncher.exe . Merge the archive's folders into the matching folders there. Install both the executables and the included db folder . Copying only the .exe files is not enough: the engine's matching scripts are included in the archive too. Clear the shader cache through the Anomaly launcher once before the first launch with the updated engine. Folder in the engine ZIP Destination bin/ Anomaly/bin/ db/ Anomaly/db/ , including its contents under db/mods/ Any other included folders The matching locations inside the Anomaly game folder. Here, Anomaly/ means your game installation directory, not a folder inside the Nemesis mod. The ZIP's db folder goes directly into that game directory. Follow the official engine installation instructions for the full package. Install with Mod Organizer 2 Install the chosen ZIP as a mod. Enable Nemesis and the required xlibs dependency. Start the game and find MCM → Nemesis Spawn System . Release archives contain gamedata/ at their root. For a manual installation, merge that directory into the game installation. See Getting started for requirements and Compatibility for optional integrations. The exact name or enabled state of xlibs in a modpack can vary; verify it in your own mod list. Updating an existing game Update between hunts . Do not update while a hunt is active, while a following squad is pending, or while the Emission Boss is on his way. Finish the encounter and save first. In Mod Organizer, install version 1.0.3 over the existing mod and choose Replace. Existing saves and saved settings carry over when updating in the supported state. The current defaults do not automatically replace your custom settings. To apply the current 1.0.3 preset balance, select Medium under Difficulty . Presets apply immediately; read the backup behaviour before switching. Removing the mod Use the uninstall procedure , especially if the save has contained an active Nemesis encounter. Removing the files alone does not remove custom squads from an active-hunt save."}, {"title": "Legends", "url": "Legends.html", "text": "A Legend is a returning hunter who has reached the highest rank. He keeps his own name and comes alone as a boss. Encounter essentials A Legend is a returning named hunter, arriving alone as a killable boss. His death permanently retires that identity. Credited kills can earn the shared boss stash reward. Reaching Legend The Returning advances a named hunter through the ranks as the player forces him to retreat. When he enters the Legend stage, he leaves ordinary hunts and waits for a separate arrival. A Legend is not an extra member of a normal squad. Natural appearance Setting Medium default Legend chance 2% Legend roll every 2 in-game hours No Legend after a boss 12 in-game hours Waiting Legends are checked at the roll interval. A Legend cannot arrive during another hunt or its warning, while another boss is alive, or during the boss lockout. A roll interval of 0 prevents natural Legend visits. The encounter A Legend arrives alone , with boss rules: he fights to the death, uses the shared guaranteed-miss setting, and can award a stash for a credited kill. Beating the boss hunt halves escalation . When a real Legend dies, that hunter's name is retired permanently. See Loot and rewards . Manual encounters Debug & tools can force a waiting Legend or trigger a roll. With no Legend waiting, those choices can create Testing Legend , a temporary stand-in who retires no real name. That stand-in is not part of normal name progression. Settings: The Returning · Boss ."}, {"title": "Loot and rewards", "url": "Loot-and-Rewards.html", "text": "When a body stays Regular squadmates and bosses can leave lootable bodies when the player receives kill credit. Named hunters normally retreat, and their bodies do not become ordinary rewards if something defeats their protection. The short assist rule also credits a kill by another NPC when it happens within two seconds of your hit. A member killed without your credit normally teleports away. A body that dies while still cloaked can disappear instead of leaving an invisible corpse. The normal boss becomes persistently visible after your shot, helping preserve the body you earn. When an uncredited body teleports away, its dropped held weapon can disappear with it if still on the ground. A weapon already picked up by someone is left alone. Rare boss stash With Boss stash reward enabled, a credited boss kill can mark a rare yellow stash . The marked reward uses a real, openable stash box and the game's stash contents rather than a fixed guaranteed item list. If the current map has no available suitable box, the reward can remain owed and be tried on a later map. The manual does not promise a particular gun, artifact or amount of money. Shared rewards apply to eligible boss encounters , including Legends . Killing a boss does not automatically clear his living escort. Protecting earned loot Reserve your kills can keep NPC looters off your credited Nemesis bodies. AlifePlus's own corresponding option takes priority when that integration is present. Related: Faction variants · Combat settings · Boss settings ."}, {"title": "Map following", "url": "Map-Following.html", "text": "A running hunt can follow you to another map. Walking through an exit, fast travel and guides can all count. Default behaviour Maps they follow you: 2. Surviving members keep their remaining health. The hunt time limit continues; travelling does not start a fresh hunt. Hunters normally approach from the side you arrived from. After arrival, you must move 40 m from the entry point before the follow countdown starts. The squad then arrives after 60–120 seconds , normally 70–140 m away. Dialogue, sleep and underground maps hold followers back. Entering a friendly base does not cancel a running follow. Escaping With the default allowance, the squad can follow twice. A further map change shakes it off and counts as an escape, lowering escalation by one cycle. Set the follow allowance to 0 to make a map change an escape. If every named hunter is already breaking off when you leave, there may be no following squad; the encounter can instead count as beaten. Boss encounters The Hunter Boss follows as part of his hunt. The Emission Boss can follow too, but does not create a new ghost ring on the destination map. Related: Hunts settings · Installation and updating ."}, {"title": "PDA messages", "url": "PDA-Messages.html", "text": "Messages announce arrival, taunts, retreats, boss warnings, returning identities and changes in The Hunt's Eye . Normal presentation Nemesis messages use its banner portrait, with an optional HD icon . Later taunts can become more personal and use your name. Arrival warning PDA is off by default. It is separate from Signs before the hunt , which belong to the Hunt's Eye. Wearable Devices PDA mod When the Wearable Devices PDA mod is installed, Messages on the Wearable Devices PDA mod , enabled by default, also sends Nemesis messages to its Messages page. This is distinct from Wearable Devices mod Malfunction , which controls screen effects. Without the optional PDA mod, the forwarding option has no additional effect. Normal message presentation remains subject to the game's interface and other UI mods. Missing messages Check the relevant setting, the optional PDA integration and whether another mod hides popup messages. The troubleshooting page explains how to collect logs if messages fail. Related settings: General · The Hunt's Eye ."}, {"title": "Psy hum", "url": "Psy-Hum.html", "text": "The nearest named hunter carries a directional psy hum. It gives you a sound cue for his position even when he is hard to see. The sound is strongest within about 10 m and fades by roughly 100 m . Only the nearest named hunter supplies the active cue, rather than every hunter playing a full-volume loop at once. The psy hum uses a mono psy-voice sound so it can be positioned in the world. This fixes the loss of direction caused by some sound replacements in earlier versions. Psy hum volume defaults to 10% on General settings . Set it to zero to mute it. The hum is an atmospheric location cue; this page does not describe it as an automatic psy-health drain. Related: Cloaking · Atmosphere · Hunters and squads ."}, {"title": "Remembered ground", "url": "Remembered-Ground.html", "text": "When your hits force a named hunter to retreat, Nemesis can remember where you were standing. What is remembered Up to five spots per map are retained, and each is forgotten after seven in-game days . A later hunt finding you near one can know where you are instead of searching your last known position. Extra hunters can take wider flanks, and a PDA line can warn you that they remember the place. The normal remembered radius is 40 m . Interaction with Targeted Ground Targeted Ground can enlarge that radius. With the default 100% increase at full targeting, the radius can reach 80 m . This is awareness around remembered spots, not an increase to every sight range in the game. Remembered ground and The Returning are related but different: one records a fighting location, while the other preserves the identity and progression of a hunter. Related: Retreats · Chasing and combat · The Hunt's Eye settings ."}, {"title": "Reserve your kills", "url": "Reserve-Your-Kills.html", "text": "Reserve your kills is enabled by default under Combat settings . It stops NPC looters from taking bodies earned through Nemesis's player kill/assist credit. Without AlifePlus A reserved body stays protected for up to two in-game hours , or until you have been more than 150 m away for three minutes . This is a reservation of the corpse for you, not a promise that no other game system can ever affect it. With AlifePlus AlifePlus's own Reserve your kills option, range and time rules govern. If its option is off, Nemesis does not impose a competing reservation through that integration. This feature does not make uncredited NPC kills into player loot. See Loot and rewards for which bodies stay. Related: Compatibility · Troubleshooting ."}, {"title": "Retreats", "url": "Retreats.html", "text": "Ordinary named hunters are designed to break off rather than die to normal hits. Their default retreat threshold is 25% health . Breaking off A sufficiently damaging hit triggers their protection and retreat. With Retreat to cover first enabled, the hunter tries to run out of sight before teleporting away. If suitable cover is unavailable, he can run away along a clear route instead. The run is limited to a few seconds before the teleport. A retreating named hunter does not become an ordinary wounded stalker waiting to be finished. Regular squadmates remain killable. What a retreat changes A retreat caused by your credited damage can: Keep the named hunter for The Returning . Add 8 Mark by default through The Mark . Increase Targeted Ground and record remembered ground . An NPC-caused retreat is not automatically credited to the player for those systems. A hunt can continue after its last named hunter leaves if regular squadmates are still alive. When the whole encounter ends, its outcome determines escalation . Bosses Bosses fight to the death rather than retreating because their health is low. A hunt's time limit can still make a boss leave. See Bosses and Loot and rewards . Settings: Retreat health · Retreat to cover first ."}, {"title": "Boss settings", "url": "Settings-Boss.html", "text": "The normal Hunter Boss uses these identity, chance and escort controls. Some shared rules, including guaranteed misses and stash rewards, also apply to other bosses. Escort zero allows a solo normal boss; chance zero disables natural normal-boss selection. See Loot and rewards . Options Current defaults are for version 1.0.3. Existing saved values can differ. Setting Default What it does Boss name Bogdan Kravets The Monolith boss's name. UNISG boss name Lyutomyr Kat The UNISG boss's name (when you play Zombified or Sin, or Monolith with UNISG picked). Military boss name Taras Hrytsenko The Military boss's name (when the Military hunt you as a Monolith player). Boss chance (%) 4 Chance that a hunt brings the boss. It grows the longer he stays away, and the 30th hunt without him brings him for sure. 0 = no boss. Boss days after grace 7 Days after the grace period before a boss can appear. Boss escort size 3 Named hunters who come with the boss. 0 = he comes alone. Boss presence distortion (%) 40 A gentle screen wave when he is near (from 60 m). 0 = off. Boss damage reduction (%) 30 How much less damage the boss takes. Boss damage against NPCs (%) 200 How hard the boss hits other stalkers and mutants. Boss guaranteed misses 2 Shared boss setting for the initial guaranteed misses against the player. See Boss encounters for its use by each encounter. 0 = none. Boss stash reward On A player-credited boss kill can mark a rare yellow stash. Kill/assist credit and rewards deferred when no suitable box is available are explained in Loot and rewards ."}, {"title": "Combat settings", "url": "Settings-Combat.html", "text": "These controls affect protection, accuracy, retreat and optional retaliation features. Read Escalation and protection before combining percentage settings. See Reserve your kills , Retreats and Thunder casting for their exceptions and interactions. Options Current defaults are for version 1.0.3. Existing saved values can differ. Setting Default What it does Retreat health (%) 25 Health at which a named hunter breaks off and teleports away. The bosses never do. Accuracy boost (%) 20 How much better they shoot than normal stalkers. Squadmates get half, the boss 15 more. 0 = off. Regular stalker damage reduction (%) 0 How much less damage the regular squadmates take. 0 = normal stalkers. Named hunter damage reduction (%) 25 How much less damage named hunters take, on top of their armour. Escalation per cycle (%) 2 How much tougher each beaten hunt makes them. 0 = no growth. Max escalation (%) 20 The most the hunts can ever add. Max damage reduction (%) 70 Caps ordinary combined damage reduction. The Thunder Boss’s casting reduction replaces the ordinary reductions and this cap while active. Halve escalation when a hunter kills you On Dying to a hunter halves the escalation cycle, never below 1. This consequence can survive reloading an older save; it does not halve every combat statistic. Hunter armour class bonus 0.05 Adds the configured armour-class bonus; this differs from percentage damage reduction. See protection rules for member types and supported ballistics handling. Hunter BR mitigation bonus (%) 10 Adds the configured bullet-resistance mitigation bonus. See protection rules for how armour and reductions interact. Reserve your kills On NPCs leave your Nemesis kills alone. With AlifePlus, its own \"reserve own kills\" option decides. Latest exe AI features On Hunters pick their target more cleverly and turn on whoever shoots them. Needs the latest modded exes. Hunter attack pull 600 How readily named hunters turn on whoever shoots them. 0 = the game decides. Boss attack pull 300 The same for the boss (kept low: he prefers you)."}, {"title": "Debug & tools settings", "url": "Settings-Debug-and-Tools.html", "text": "Player-accessible diagnostic and encounter controls. Debug mode changes encounter rules/timing; Debug logs adds information for bug reports . Both are off by default. Enable logs alone when collecting an ordinary-play report. Prepare for uninstall is a removal procedure, not a difficulty option. Options Current defaults are for version 1.0.3. Existing saved values can differ. Setting Default What it does Debug mode Off For testing: hunts come quickly (15 s after the warning) and can start anywhere, and a map marker follows the boss. Debug logs Off Writes every Nemesis line to its own log file, appdata/logs/nemesis.log , for bug reports. Force the next hunt None For testing: Boss brings the boss in the next hunt; Emission boss brings him now; A returning hunter leads the next hunt; A Legend comes now; The Legends' roll happens now; Thunder Boss brings him once no hunt or boss is active. If no Legend is waiting, either Legend choice brings the temporary Testing Legend; his death retires no real name. Goes back to None. Reset escalation now – Tick to set the hunters back to their weakest right away. Prepare for uninstall Off Ends any hunt and removes every Nemesis squad, so you can save and uninstall safely. No hunts while ticked. Forced encounters Force the next hunt acts once and returns to None. A boss selection is not a promise of a second simultaneous hunt. Legend selections can create Testing Legend if no real Legend waits; that temporary encounter retires no real name. Use Reset escalation now deliberately: it immediately returns the hunters to their weakest cycle. It does not stand in for the full uninstall procedure ."}, {"title": "Emission Boss settings", "url": "Settings-Emission-Boss.html", "text": "Controls for the Emission Boss , his ghosts and the horde . Main chance is the replacement roll when a hunt meets an emission; Extra chance is the separate opportunity without a hunt. Horde size zero disables the group; flank share zero keeps it on the boss's side. Options Current defaults are for version 1.0.3. Existing saved values can differ. Setting Default What it does Emission boss On Turns the emission boss on or off. Emission boss chance (%) 100 Chance, once per emission, that the emission boss replaces the hunt. 0 = off. Emission boss extra chance (%) 6 Chance, once per emission, that he comes with no hunt at all. 0 = off. Emission boss name Ilarion Hrech The emission boss's name. Emission boss damage reduction (%) 30 How much less damage he takes. Emission boss NPC damage reduction (%) 70 How much less damage other stalkers and mutants do to him. Emission boss horde size 6 Horde members that approach 20 seconds before him: zombified stalkers, with snorks according to the area. 0 = none. See The emission horde . Horde flank share (%) 30 How much of the horde comes from the other side of your shelter, when it has two doors or more. 0 = all from his side. Ghost psy damage (%) 4 The share of your psy bar you lose each time the ghosts push you back, whatever you wear. 0 = none."}, {"title": "General settings", "url": "Settings-General.html", "text": "General contains difficulty selection, backup restore, arrival warnings, sound/effect controls and optional device integrations. Read Difficulty and presets for immediate application and backup behaviour. Related features: Atmosphere , Psy hum , Boss heartbeat , PDA messages . Options Current defaults are for version 1.0.3. Existing saved values can differ. Setting Default What it does Difficulty Medium Easy, Medium or Hard; immediately applies the values listed in Difficulty and presets . Cancel does not undo the selection. Load settings from Automatic Backup – Puts back your own settings from before a difficulty replaced them, unapplied ones too, so it also undoes a pick. The box unticks itself. HD icon Off Sharper portrait on the PDA messages. Arrival warning audio On Radio static shortly before a hunt. Arrival warning PDA Off A PDA message from an unknown frequency shortly before a hunt. Arrival warning volume 70 Loudness of the warning static. Teleport sound volume 50 Loudness of the teleport sound. 0 mutes it. Psy hum volume 10 Loudness of the psy hum the nearest named hunter carries. Lightning volume 60 Every lightning in the mod: the strike when the emission boss arrives, and the Thunder Boss's thunder and bolts. Heartbeat volume (%) 60 The heartbeat near the boss. 0 = off. Heartbeat top rate (bpm) 140 How fast it beats when he is on you (120 to 180). Arrival call volume (%) 50 The Monolith's call where a hunt lands. 0 = off. Arrival screen effect (%) 40 The psy swell when a hunt lands. 0 = off. Arrival escalation per cycle (%) 10 How much heavier the arrival gets each cycle. Arrival maximum increase (%) 50 The cap on that. Silence before the warning On The world goes quiet before the radio warning. Silence length (s) 1 The dead air before the radio (0.5 to 1). Wearable Devices mod Malfunction On With the Wearable Devices mod: your device screens flicker when a hunt spawns, and go black for 2 s when the boss comes. Messages on the Wearable Devices PDA mod On With the Wearable Devices PDA mod (which hides the game's message popups): every Nemesis message also goes to its Messages page."}, {"title": "Hunts settings", "url": "Settings-Hunts.html", "text": "These controls set ordinary hunt timing, placement, composition and pursuit. See Hunts , Hunters and squads , Map following , Cloaking and Faction variants . Grace, cooldown and hunt duration use in-game time. Squad-size values are relative weights, not percentages. Boss escort size is separate. Options Current defaults are for version 1.0.3. Existing saved values can differ. Setting Default What it does Grace period (days) 8 Days of peace before the first hunt of a new game. Time between hunts (hours) 24 Rest time after a hunt before the next one. Hunt time limit (hours) 6 Survive this long and the hunters give up. Spawn distance, min (m) 80 Closest a hunt can spawn to you. Spawn distance, max (m) 140 Farthest a hunt can spawn from you. Squad of 1 to 5, weight 50 / 40 / 25 / 20 / 10 How often each squad size appears. They don't need to add up to 100. Extra is a named hunter (%) 25 Base chance for each extra member after the named leader to be another named hunter. Area scaling can adjust this chance. Scale hunts by area On Smaller squads in rookie areas, more named hunters in the far north. Hunt your last known position On When he loses you, he searches where he last saw or heard you. Off: he always heads for where you are. Squad tactics On Squadmates flank you. Retreat to cover first On A hurt hunter runs for cover before teleporting away. Maps they follow you 2 How many times a hunt follows you to another map. 0 = never (changing maps is an escape). Hunter cloak (experimental) On Named hunters turn invisible while stalking you from far away. Fight range (m) 100 When they see you within this distance, they stop chasing and fight. Monolith players are hunted by Military Selects Military or UNISG hunters for Monolith players from the next hunt onward. The Emission Boss uses the same faction selection."}, {"title": "The Hunt's Eye settings", "url": "Settings-The-Hunts-Eye.html", "text": "Controls for Signs , The Mark and Targeted Ground . Mark sleep/medicine effects remove a share of the current Mark; targeting drops remove meter points. The combined hunt-interval reduction is capped at 50%. Options Current defaults are for version 1.0.3. Existing saved values can differ. Setting Default What it does The Hunt's Eye On Signs before the hunt, the Mark and Targeted Ground. Off: none of them. Signs lead (hours) 1 In-game hours between the chatter on your PDA and the hunt. 0 = no signs. Mark per boss kill 20 How much the Mark grows when you kill a boss, the emission boss or a Legend (of 100). Mark per named hunter kill 10 The same for a named hunter you kill. Mark per squad member kill 2 The same for a regular hunt squad member. Other stalkers add nothing. Mark per forced retreat 8 How much the Mark grows when your hits make a named hunter break off. Mark: hunts sooner (%) 30 How much sooner the next hunt comes at a full Mark. Mark fades over (hours) 48 In-game hours for a full Mark to fade by itself. 0 = never. Sleep eases the Mark (%) 50 How much of the Mark a sleep takes away. Anti-psy eases the Mark (%) 35 How much of the Mark an anti-psy drug takes away. Targeting per hunt 15 How much a map's targeting grows with every hunt on it (of 100). Targeting per forced retreat 10 How much it grows when a named hunter gets away from you there. Targeting per stay 10 How much it grows every Targeting stay hours you stay on the map. Targeting stay (hours) 12 In-game hours for that step. Cooling rate (x) 3 How much faster a map cools while you are away than it grows while you stay. Targeting drop per map travel 20 How much every map's targeting drops when you change maps. Targeting drop per anti-psy 30 How much this map's targeting drops with an anti-psy drug. Targeting: hunts sooner (%) 25 How much sooner hunts come on a fully targeted map. With the Mark, never more than half. Targeting: they know your ground (%) 100 How much further away they know your old spots on a fully targeted map."}, {"title": "The Returning settings", "url": "Settings-The-Returning.html", "text": "Controls for returning identities , rank effects and Legends . They are part of difficulty presets . Rank effects by is relevant with AlifeTactics installed; without it, Nemesis's rank effects are used. Legend interval and lockout use in-game hours. Interval zero stops natural Legend visits. Options Current defaults are for version 1.0.3. Existing saved values can differ. Setting Default What it does The Returning On Named hunters who break off from you come back by name, one rank higher each time. Off: random titles, as before. Return chance (%) 35 Chance that a hunt is led by a hunter who broke off from you before. Rank effects by AlifeTactics Only with AlifeTactics installed: AlifeTactics' own skill curves, or Nemesis's sliders below. Accuracy per rank (%) 4 Nemesis's rank effects: more of his shots hit, per rank above Experienced. Vision per rank (%) 4 He notices you faster and from further away, per rank (latest exes). Bursts per rank (%) 5 His bursts are longer, per rank (latest exes). Legend chance (%) 2 Chance that a waiting Legend comes for you, at each roll. Legend roll every (hours) 2 In-game hours between natural Legend rolls. 0 disables natural visits; forced encounters are separate. No Legend after a boss (hours) 12 No Legend this soon after any boss was in the world."}, {"title": "Thunder Boss settings", "url": "Settings-Thunder-Boss.html", "text": "Controls for Danylo Raskat , independent of difficulty presets. Lightning volume is on General . Casting reduction replaces ordinary reductions and their cap. Regeneration uses full health per second; bolt damage uses the player's current health per reaching bolt. Options Current defaults are for version 1.0.3. Existing saved values can differ. Setting Default What it does Thunder Boss On Enables his visits and lightning. Thunder Boss name Danylo Raskat His displayed name. Thunder Boss chance per storm (%) 15 One roll per eligible thunderstorm. 0 stops natural visits. Days between his visits 5 Minimum in-game days between natural visits. Thunder Boss damage reduction (%) 90 Damage reduction while casting; replaces other reductions and their cap. Lightning damage (%) 7 Current health lost per bolt that reaches you, through armour. Regeneration while casting (%/s) 4 Percentage of maximum health restored each real second while his hand is raised. Default 4; range 0–10. Set 0 to disable healing. It stops when the cast ends. Lightning range (m) 30 Maximum distance for lightning aimed at you. Lightning range at NPCs (m) 45 Maximum distance for lightning aimed at enemy stalkers or mutants. Thunder Boss NPC damage reduction (%) 60 Damage reduction against NPCs and mutants at all times. Understanding regeneration The default value 4 restores 4% of maximum health per real second while Thunder has his hand raised. It is not a percentage of his remaining health and is not permanent healing between casts. Five seconds of uninterrupted healing restores 20 percentage points, capped at full health. Incoming damage can reduce the net gain. Set the slider to 0 to stop this healing without turning off his lightning encounter. Read Health regeneration and casting protection for how it affects the fight."}, {"title": "Settings", "url": "Settings.html", "text": "Open MCM → Nemesis Spawn System to configure the mod. Pages Page Controls General Difficulty, backup, warning audio, volumes, screen effects and device support. Hunts Grace, interval, duration, spawn range, composition, cloak, travel and hunter faction. Combat Accuracy, retreat, armour/reductions, escalation, retaliation and body reservation. Boss Normal boss names, chance, escorts and shared boss options. Emission Boss Emission rolls, damage reduction, ghosts and horde. The Returning Returns, rank effects and Legend scheduling. The Hunt's Eye Signs, Mark gains/decay and map targeting. Thunder Boss Storm visits, lightning, casting reduction and regeneration. Debug & tools Diagnostic logs, forced encounters, escalation reset and safe removal. The Difficulty and presets page compares Easy, Medium and Hard side by side. Reading the values This manual lists the current defaults for version 1.0.3 . Existing saves can retain different values. Ordinary option edits use MCM's normal application behaviour; preset selection is special because it applies immediately. Day/hour labels on hunt scheduling refer to in-game time . Short combat, warning and cast delays described in seconds refer to encounter timing. Follow the units printed beside an option rather than treating every number as real hours. Percentages can mean different things. Thunder regeneration is based on full health, his bolt damage on current health, and an anti-psy item's Mark effect removes a share of the current Mark. Each system's page explains its unit. Debug mode and Debug logs are off by default."}, {"title": "Sieges", "url": "Sieges.html", "text": "Hiding in a building changes how a hunt approaches. Hunters can wait outside rather than immediately entering after you. Waiting and storming in Situation Default wait Ordinary hunt 90 seconds indoors. Hunter Boss escort 20 seconds indoors. You shoot a hunter from inside The wait ends immediately. Hunters spread to positions outside and can fight other threats while waiting. After the wait, they push in. Briefly crossing a doorway does not repeatedly buy a fresh siege timer: the ordinary reset requires about 10 seconds outside . A small awning is not a reliable way to reset the encounter. Bosses and shelters The Hunter Boss does not wait with his escort; his indoor approach heads toward you. The Emission Boss's ghosts require a real emission-safe shelter, which is a different check from ordinary indoor siege behaviour. A roof alone does not guarantee that every shelter-related feature treats a place as a building. Other map geometry and shelter rules still matter. Related: Chasing and combat · Ghosts · Hunts ."}, {"title": "Signs before the hunt", "url": "Signs-Before-the-Hunt.html", "text": "Signs are intercepted chatter on your PDA before an ordinary hunt. They give you time to prepare and indicate which faction is coming. Lead time The default Signs lead is one in-game hour . The hunt waits until that lead time has elapsed. Set it to 0 to disable Signs. Signs are part of The Hunt's Eye . Turning off that system disables them too. Signs versus arrival warnings Warning Timing and control Sign Early intercepted chatter, controlled by Signs lead. Arrival audio Radio static shortly before the squad arrives; enabled by default. Arrival PDA warning An additional short arrival warning; disabled by default. Turning off the optional arrival PDA warning does not by itself turn off Signs. Likewise, a Sign does not mean the squad is already standing nearby. Debug mode still shows the Sign but bypasses the normal wait; see Debug & tools settings . Related: Hunts · PDA messages · The Hunt's Eye settings ."}, {"title": "Targeted Ground", "url": "Targeted-Ground.html", "text": "Targeted Ground is a 0–100 meter for each map . Fighting hunts in one area and staying there too long makes that map more heavily targeted. Building pressure Event Medium default Hunt fought on the map +15 Named hunter forced to retreat there by your hits +10 Staying on the map +10 every 12 in-game hours Effects At full targeting, hunts come 25% sooner by default. With The Mark , the combined reduction to the hunt interval is capped at 50%. Targeting also expands remembered ground . The default full-targeting bonus is 100% , doubling the remembered-spot radius from 40 m to 80 m. This is not a global vision increase for every NPC. Targeted Ground does not directly make squads larger or armour stronger. Cooling a map Maps cool while you are elsewhere, at a default rate three times the staying-related growth rate. Each map change drops targeting on every map by 20 points . An anti-psy item drops targeting on your current map by 30 points . These targeting drops are points on the map meter. They differ from the Mark's percentage-of-current-value reductions. PDA messages announce when the current map reaches the targeted threshold of 50 and when it cools to 25 . Related: The Hunt's Eye · Map following · The Hunt's Eye settings ."}, {"title": "The Emission Boss", "url": "The-Emission-Boss.html", "text": "Ilarion Hrech is an emission-linked boss who can surround your shelter with ghosts before arriving with a horde. Encounter essentials Stay in a real emission-safe shelter; the ghost ring does not protect you from the emission. Expect the horde before the boss, with possible pressure from multiple exits. The boss does not cloak, and changing maps does not create another ghost ring. When he appears When an emission meets a running or due hunt, the default 100% Emission boss chance can replace it with this encounter. A separate 6% extra chance per emission can bring him without a hunt, subject to the grace period and normal safety gates. For most player factions he is Sin. For Monolith players he follows the Military/UNISG hunter selection; Zombified and Sin players receive UNISG. His display name is separately configurable. He does not start a second encounter while another boss is active. Encounter sequence The dangerous part of the emission begins. Get into a building that is a real emission-safe shelter and remain there briefly. Ghosts form a ring and guard exits. The horde approaches before the boss. He arrives in lightning as the event's effects fade, then comes for you. If you never get indoors within about 60 seconds , he can arrive without the ghost setup. Staying outside still leaves you exposed to the emission itself. His arrival follows the emission's own progression, so it should not be treated as a simple fixed stopwatch from the first warning. The ghost encounter lasts at least 20 seconds before his arrival; the horde is scheduled 20 seconds before him . Fighting him He walks on his approach, does not cloak or sneak, and knows where you are. The emission spares him without making all other members of his faction immune. Setting Medium default Boss damage reduction 30% Damage reduction against NPCs/mutants 70% Horde size 6 Horde flank share 30% Ghost psy damage 4% of the full psy bar per eligible push He and his horde do not fight each other. Shared boss guaranteed misses and stash rewards apply. He fights to the death, although the hunt can still end through its normal limits. Ghosts remain until his hunt ends or you change maps; if he follows, the destination does not get a new ring. Reload taunt Reload while he is alive within 50 m , and he can respond with his own PDA taunt. The shared boss rule allows this at most once per hunt . Related: Ghosts · The emission horde · Map following · Emission Boss settings ."}, {"title": "The emission horde", "url": "The-Emission-Horde.html", "text": "The horde approaches shortly before the Emission Boss . Its default size is six , and its arrival is scheduled 20 seconds before the boss . Composition and placement The group uses zombified stalkers, with snorks added according to the area's danger tier. Middle-tier areas can include one snork; high-tier areas can include two tougher snorks. With ghosts present, the horde forms outdoors beyond the ring, on the boss's side. In a shelter with at least two detected doors, 30% flank share can send a portion from the other side, rather than bringing everyone through the same direction. Without the haunting, the horde approaches along the route toward the boss's arrival instead. Combat Horde members can fight visible hostile stalkers and mutants within 40 m along the way. That describes the horde's own willingness to fight; it does not force every other NPC to ignore their own combat restrictions. The horde and Emission Boss are allies. If the boss cannot arrive, the group is released from the encounter's control. Horde size 0 disables the horde. Flank share 0 brings it from the boss's side. Related: Ghosts · Emission Boss settings ."}, {"title": "The Hunter Boss", "url": "The-Hunter-Boss.html", "text": "The Hunter Boss is a rare, stronger hunter with a cloaked approach, named escorts and a fight to the death. Encounter essentials Your shot breaks his cloaked approach and keeps him visible for the hunt. Listen for the heartbeat and watch for nearby screen distortion. His named escort can keep hunting after he dies. Identity Hunter faction Default name Monolith Bogdan Kravets UNISG Lyutomyr Kat Military Taras Hrytsenko Names are editable in Boss settings . See Faction variants for who hunts your faction. Arrival The initial default boss chance is 4% per hunt . Every four hunts without him adds two percentage points, and the 30th hunt without him guarantees him. A zero boss chance disables him. By default he is unavailable until seven in-game days after the grace period . Normal boss scheduling also limits repeat appearances and does not allow another boss already alive in the world. His default escort is three named hunters , separate from normal squad-size weights. Escort size zero makes him arrive alone. The boss and escort teleport in with arrival effects. The approach If you stand still, walk or crouch, he can circle toward your back while cloaked. Once behind you, he closes in rather than circling indefinitely. If you run, he can rush directly toward you instead. Within about 20 m , his warning and attack delay depend on that approach: the behind-you sneak has a longer warning, while the rushing attack uses about 1.5 seconds . Reaching within 10 m also triggers the close approach's short warning. A shot from you breaks the approach and keeps him visible for the rest of the hunt; simply seeing him does not. After his approach ends, the combat AI chooses threats normally. He can answer nearby NPCs shooting him rather than being permanently locked to you. Fighting and rewards His normal damage reduction is 30% , alongside applicable armour and escalation rules. He does 200% damage to other NPCs and mutants by default. His first two hits on the player are guaranteed misses under the shared boss setting. He does not retreat because of low health. A player-credited kill leaves his body and can award a rare stash. His escort continues fighting after he dies, so finish or escape the encounter. Beating the boss hunt halves escalation . Reload taunt Reload while the boss is alive and within 50 m , and he can answer with a PDA taunt. This happens at most once per hunt ; it is an encounter message, not a separate reload-damage effect. Listen for his heartbeat and watch for presence distortion . See Loot and rewards for credit and Cloaking for visibility rules."}, {"title": "The Hunt's Eye", "url": "The-Hunts-Eye.html", "text": "The Hunt's Eye combines three systems that make the next hunt more predictable, but can also bring it sooner. System What it tracks Signs before the hunt An early PDA warning from the faction coming for you. The Mark Pressure attached to your actions against Nemesis hunters. Targeted Ground Pressure attached to a particular map. Shared limits The Mark and Targeted Ground can shorten Time between hunts , but together cannot reduce it by more than 50% . They do not directly increase squad size, damage reduction or escalation . For example, a configured 24-hour cooldown cannot be reduced below 12 hours by these two pressure effects alone. Other arrival conditions still apply. Switching it off The Hunt's Eye has its own enable switch. Turning it off disables its effects while retaining its meters for later use. It is not a command to erase your pressure history. All three systems are explained on their own pages. Their controls appear together on The Hunt's Eye settings ."}, {"title": "The Mark", "url": "The-Mark.html", "text": "The Mark is a 0–100 pressure meter attached to you. Your actions against Nemesis hunters raise it, making later hunts come sooner. What raises it Credited action Medium default gain Kill a boss, including a Legend +20 Kill a named hunter +10 Force a named hunter to retreat +8 Kill a regular hunt-squad member +2 Other stalkers add nothing. The player's kill/assist attribution matters; a nearby NPC fighting a hunter is not automatically credited to you. The Mark is based on what you do to the hunters, not simply on how much damage they deal to you. Effect on hunt timing At a full Mark, the default timing reduction is 30% , with a proportionally smaller effect at lower values. Combined with Targeted Ground , the total cooldown reduction is capped at 50% . The Mark does not directly increase hunter armour, squad size or escalation. Reducing it Method Medium default Time A full Mark fades over 48 in-game hours. Sleep Removes 50% of the current Mark. Anti-psy item Removes 35% of the current Mark. An item with psy protection can count, including Psy-Block. At Mark 80, the default anti-psy reduction removes 28 and leaves 52; it does not subtract a fixed 35 points. The Mark fades over value can be set to 0 to stop natural fading. PDA messages announce its onset, strong pressure and disappearance. Related: Retreats · Loot credit · The Hunt's Eye settings ."}, {"title": "The Returning", "url": "The-Returning.html", "text": "The Returning lets named hunters survive in the story of your save: a hunter you drive away can return by name, at a higher title and rank, remembering where you met. How a return is earned A fresh named hunter starts at the lowest faction title and the game's Experienced rank. When your credited damage makes him break off, his identity and meeting map can be kept for a later hunt. The default Return chance is 35% for an eligible hunt's leader. Returning is not guaranteed on the very next hunt. An ordinary NPC causing the retreat does not automatically earn you a return. Rank progression Stage Game rank Fresh hunter Experienced Later return Professional Later return Veteran Later return Expert Later return Master Top stage Legend A hunter entering the top stage leaves ordinary hunts and waits for a separate Legend encounter . He no longer appears as just another ordinary named hunter. AlifeTactics , when installed and selected, provides rank-based skill changes. Otherwise Nemesis's rank sliders add accuracy, vision and burst effects. This progression does not replace escalation or directly invent extra armour per rank. Permanent retirement A tracked hunter who dies has his name retired. A retreat keeps him in the story; a death ends that identity. Ordinary damage protection means named hunters normally retreat, while Legends can be killed in their boss encounter. Turning The Returning off uses random named-hunter title selection instead of Returning progression. Its controls are on The Returning settings , and are included in difficulty presets."}, {"title": "The Thunder Boss", "url": "The-Thunder-Boss.html", "text": "Danylo Raskat is a rare storm visitor who arrives alone in lightning. He wears an Exo-SEVA Loner appearance with electric sparks around his legs, and has no cloaked sneak approach. While his hand is raised, he regenerates 4% of his maximum health every second by default. Attack between casts to avoid fighting through both his healing and casting protection. Encounter essentials Keep moving: player-directed bolts aim at your recent position. Attack between casts; a raised hand means healing and casting protection. Healing restores maximum-health percentage points, while bolt damage removes a share of your current health. Natural visits Condition Default Chance per eligible thunderstorm 15% Minimum gap 5 in-game days First encounter prerequisite At least one hunt has happened. Competing encounters No other active hunt or boss. Emission/psy-storm exclusion During the event and for 5 real minutes afterward. His natural arrival is tied to a real thunderstorm. Rain alone is not a guarantee. Chance 0 stops natural visits; his enable switch controls the encounter. Read the cast His sparks intensify and a buzz gives about 1.5 seconds of warning before the raised-hand sequence. He puts away his weapon and raises a hand. The first volley follows about 1.5 seconds after the hand is raised. A full cast has three volleys , normally three seconds apart , with two to five bolts per volley . After the cast ends, at least 20 seconds pass before the next cast can begin. A missing valid target can end a cast early rather than leaving him casting at nothing. Player-directed bolts aim at where you were about half a second earlier . A bolt reaching within about one metre of its landing point can hurt you. Keep moving instead of trying to out-tank the whole sequence. Health regeneration and casting protection Thunder heals only while his hand is raised during a cast. The default rate is 4% of maximum health per real second , rather than 4% of his remaining health. The initial warning charge before his hand goes up does not give him this healing. For example, if he starts at 40% health and spends five seconds with his hand raised, he can recover 20 percentage points and reach 60%, provided he takes no damage during that time. Healing cannot take him above full health. While his hand is raised, his default 90% casting damage reduction replaces the ordinary reductions and their cap. The regeneration and casting protection end when the cast ends; he does not keep this regeneration during ordinary gun combat. This makes the gaps between casts the main opportunity to hurt him. Regeneration while casting (%/s) controls the healing rate. Its range is 0–10% of maximum health per second; 0 disables this healing . The default is 4, and difficulty presets do not change it. Targets and damage Setting Default Lightning range at the player 30 m Lightning range at enemy NPCs/mutants 45 m Player health lost per reaching bolt 7% of current health Casting damage reduction 90% Regeneration while hand is raised 4% of full health per second NPC/mutant damage reduction 60% at all times A wall between Thunder and the player can block a bolt's damage when the cover check detects the obstruction. Reaching the landing point alone is not enough to damage the player through that wall. Armour alone does not block the health drain. Lightning bypasses ordinary armour protection. NPC volleys can kill their target on the final bolt. Story/protected NPCs, companions, traders and Nemesis hunters are excluded from his lightning targets. The first volley can target you when he sees you within range. Later volleys favour suitable nearby NPC enemies; if none exists, they can target you again. This is not a guarantee of one NPC death on every volley. His casting reduction replaces the ordinary reductions and their cap while active. Attack between casts, when he is not receiving that casting protection and regeneration. Suppression after casting When a cast ends, Thunder has a 50% chance to enter a 2.5-second suppression burst . His weapon bursts become longer where the supported engine feature is available, while his shot spread becomes much wider. It is intentionally inaccurate suppressive fire, not a guaranteed precision attack. He can return to gun combat while his lightning cooldown runs. The next cast cannot begin until at least 20 seconds after the previous cast ended , and it still needs an eligible visible target. If the target is gone when his warning charge finishes, that charge can fade without becoming a cast. Appearance, death and travel His arrival uses five sky strikes at random moments within 0.7 seconds . An uncredited NPC kill uses the same lightning departure around his position instead of leaving a body. A player-credited kill leaves his body without that departure sequence. Loot credit includes the short player-assist window. His settings are independent of difficulty presets . Lightning volume is on General settings ; his combat controls are on Thunder Boss settings ."}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "Troubleshooting.html", "text": "No hunt is starting Check the grace period and cooldown , whether you are underground/asleep/in dialogue, and whether you are near a friendly or neutral base. Check that Prepare for uninstall is off. A hunt or following squad already pending prevents another ordinary hunt from starting. Debug mode changes these conditions and timings; it is off by default. Use Debug logs to investigate ordinary play rather than assuming silence means the mod is broken. A named hunter died instead of retreating Verify both the modded executable and its matching scripts , including conflict winners. The required callback support dates from 15 September 2026. Updating the executable without restoring the correct scripts can leave ballistics protection unavailable. Confirm the NPC type: regular squadmates and bosses are meant to be killable. See Hunters and squads . A body vanished An uncredited NPC kill normally teleports away. Named hunters do not leave ordinary lootable bodies, and a still-cloaked death can disappear to avoid an invisible corpse. See Loot and rewards . A player assist has a short two-second window. If you are unsure whether the kill was credited, report the encounter with logs. A message or cloak is missing Check PDA settings , optional UI integrations and the invisible-model requirement . Turn up the relevant sound/effect setting before treating an absent cue as a failure. Recognising log lines Message Meaning NEMESIS IMPORTANT ERROR (please report) Report it with both logs; it can indicate an install or lasting-state problem. xlibs requirement / mod off Check xlibs installation, version and conflicts. Missing squad section Check Nemesis configuration files and conflict resolution. release guard (expected) Can be normal guarded squad cleanup; this line alone is not a broken hunt. Member-unregistered line when quitting Can be normal level unloading; timing matters. Reporting a problem Enable Debug logs in MCM → Nemesis Spawn System → Debug & tools. Reproduce the problem if possible. Send both appdata/logs/nemesis.log and appdata/logs/xray_<name>.log . Include the Nemesis version/package, modpack, engine build, xlibs version and what happened. Mention a recent load/map change, the boss or hunt involved, and any crash report. Important errors can appear even with Debug logs off. Ordinary diagnostics need it enabled. Player and commented-source packages can have different script line numbers, so include the exact package name. See Installation and updating and Uninstalling before changing versions or removing an active encounter."}, {"title": "Uninstalling", "url": "Uninstalling.html", "text": "A save made during a hunt can contain Nemesis-specific squads. Without the mod, the game may be unable to load those squads and the save can fail to load. Recommended procedure Open MCM → Nemesis Spawn System → Debug & tools . Tick Prepare for uninstall and apply. Wait for the PDA message saying it is safe to save; the documented wait is about 10 seconds . Save the game. Disable or remove Nemesis, then load that save. Prepare for uninstall ends the encounter and clears Nemesis squads. No new hunt starts while the option remains enabled. If you decide to keep playing with Nemesis, untick it to allow hunts again. A save made cleanly between hunts is preferable to removing the mod during an encounter. Do not confuse an empty-looking area with confirmation that a following squad or boss arrival is no longer pending. Settings files Nemesis can keep gamedata/configs/nwss_player.ltx , containing its death record and your settings backup. Under Mod Organizer it may be in overwrite. Older versions may have left gamedata/nemesis_death_reset.txt . These files are separate from the squads stored in the save; removing a settings file does not make an active-hunt save safe to load without Nemesis. Related: Installation and updating · Debug and tools settings · Troubleshooting ."}, {"title": "What is new in 1.0.3", "url": "Whats-New-in-1.0.3.html", "text": "This page summarizes the current version, 1.0.3. For the complete public history, see the Changelog . New encounters and progression The Thunder Boss : rare thunderstorm visits, lightning travel, warning charges and volleys, casting protection and regeneration. The Returning : named hunters remember forced retreats, return stronger and retain their identities. Legends : top-rank hunters become solo boss encounters. The Hunt's Eye : early Signs, the player Mark and map-specific Targeted Ground. Military hunts for Monolith players , with UNISG available as an option. Settings and presentation Presets apply immediately. Automatic Backup can restore the custom settings replaced by a preset, including pending slider edits. The directional psy hum , optional Wearable Devices PDA messages , and Nemesis's own log file improve feedback and reporting. Medium defaults Setting Current default (1.0.3) Grace period 8 in-game days Time between hunts 24 in-game hours Hunt time limit 6 in-game hours Retreat health 25% Accuracy boost 20% Boss escort 3 Emission horde 6 Anti-psy Mark reduction 35% of current Mark Thunder casting regeneration 4% of full health per second Debug mode / Debug logs Both off See Settings for the complete values. Existing saved settings are not automatically reset. Fixes and refinements Version 1.0.3 includes the boss-death crash repair and subsequent combat, lightning-cast, body/weapon cleanup and Interaction Dot Marks fixes. The old boss jolt was removed. Expected release-guard messages no longer look like critical failures. These changes are described as player behaviour here; they are not a guarantee that no other mod or installation can cause a problem. See Troubleshooting ."}, {"title": "Nemesis Spawn System — Field Manual", "url": "index.html", "text": "Welcome to the English Field Manual for Nemesis Spawn System (NSS) for S.T.A.L.K.E.R. Anomaly and GAMMA. The current version covered by this manual is 1.0.3 . Nemesis adds recurring hunters who track you across the Zone. Named hunters retreat and return stronger, bosses bring distinct encounters, and your actions affect how soon the next hunt begins. Start here Getting started : requirements, the first hunt and the main survival rules. Installation and updating · Uninstalling Hunts · Hunters and squads · Faction variants What is new in 1.0.3 Changelog : the full public version history. Boss encounters Encounter What makes it different The Hunter Boss A cloaked approach, named escorts and a fight to the death. The Emission Boss Shelter ghosts, an approaching horde and a boss arriving through an emission. The Thunder Boss A storm-gated visitor with lightning volleys and health regeneration while casting. Legends Returning hunters who reach the highest rank and come back alone as bosses. Features Topic Pages Fighting and survival Chasing and combat , Cloaking , Retreats , Escalation and protection Position and travel Sieges , Remembered ground , Map following Names and progression The Returning , Legends Pressure The Hunt's Eye , Signs , The Mark , Targeted Ground Emission encounters Ghosts , The emission horde Rewards Loot and rewards , Reserve your kills Atmosphere Sound and screen effects , Psy hum , Boss heartbeat , PDA messages Settings and help Settings · Difficulty and presets · Compatibility · Troubleshooting · FAQ · Credits Default values are for the current version, 1.0.3, using Medium unless a page says otherwise. Existing saved settings can differ. Encounter pages explain boss and hunt mechanics."}];
